# Module 1: DOM Selection & Event Listeners
**Project:** Kijani Trails Travel Agency  
**Feature Goal:** Build an interactive "Plan Your Tour" Modal Popup (`#tour-modal`) that opens and closes on user interaction.

---

## 1. Prerequisites: Connecting JavaScript to HTML

Before writing any JavaScript, ensure your `index.js` file is linked at the **very bottom** of your `index.html` file, right above the closing `</body>` tag:

```html
    <!-- Link JS at the bottom so HTML elements load first -->
    <script src="index.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

> **Why at the bottom?** The browser reads HTML from top to bottom. If `<script src="index.js"></script>` is placed in the `<head>`, JavaScript will run before the buttons and modal exist on the page, causing `null` errors.

---

## 2. The Core Mental Model: The 3-Step Formula

Almost every frontend JavaScript feature follows the same three steps:

1. **SELECT:** Grab the HTML element from the page and store it in a variable.
2. **LISTEN:** Wait for the user to perform an action (e.g., clicking a button or pressing a key).
3. **REACT:** Change the element's CSS styles, classes, or text content.

```text
[ 1. Select Element ] ───► [ 2. Listen for 'click' ] ───► [ 3. Change CSS display ]
  #plan-tour-btn             addEventListener()             modal.style.display = 'flex'
```

---

## 3. Key JavaScript Concepts & Syntax

| Concept / Method | Syntax | What It Does |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`const` Variable** | `const btn = ...` | Creates a constant container in memory to store a reference to an HTML element. |
| **`getElementById()`** | `document.getElementById('id-name')` | Searches the HTML document and returns the single element matching that exact `id`. |
| **`querySelector()`** | `document.querySelector('.class-name')` | Returns the **first** HTML element that matches any CSS selector (`#id`, `.class`, or `tag`). |
| **`addEventListener()`** | `element.addEventListener('click', () => {})` | Attaches a "watcher" to an element that runs a function whenever the specified event happens. |
| **Inline Style Manipulation** | `element.style.property = 'value'` | Updates the inline CSS of an element directly from JavaScript. |

